       No  wide  character  counterpart  functions  are  defined by the “wide”
       _n_c_u_r_s_e_s configuration nor by any standard.  They  are  unnecessary:  to
       retrieve  and  convert  a  wide-character string from a _c_u_r_s_e_s terminal
       keyboard, use these functions with the _s_c_a_n_f(3) conversions  “%lc”  and
       “%ls” for wide characters and strings, respectively.

       _n_c_u_r_s_e_s  implements _v_s_s_c_a_n_f(3) internally if it is unavailable when the
       library is configured.

PPOORRTTAABBIILLIITTYY
       X/Open Curses, Issue 4 describes these functions.  It specifies no  er‐
       ror conditions for them.

       _n_c_u_r_s_e_s  defines vvww__ssccaannww and vvwwssccaannww identically to support legacy ap‐
       plications.  However, the latter is obsolete.

       •   X/Open Curses, Issue 4 Version 2 (1996), marked vvwwssccaannww as  requir‐
           ing  _v_a_r_a_r_g_s_._h  and “TO BE WITHDRAWN”, and specified vvww__ssccaannww using
           the _s_t_d_a_r_g_._h interface.

       •   X/Open Curses, Issue 5, Draft  2  (December  2007)  marked  vvwwssccaannww
           (along with vvwwssccaannww and the _t_e_r_m_c_a_p interface) as withdrawn.  After
           incorporating review comments, this became X/Open Curses,  Issue  7
           (2009).

       •   _n_c_u_r_s_e_s provides vvwwssccaannww, but marks it as deprecated.

       X/Open Curses Issues 4 and 7 both state that these functions return EERRRR
       or OOKK.  This is likely an erratum.

       •   Since the underlying _s_c_a_n_f(3) returns the number of successful con‐
           versions,  and SVr4 _c_u_r_s_e_s was documented to use this feature, this
           may have been an editorial solecism introduced  by  X/Open,  rather
           than an intentional change.

       •   This  implementation retains compatibility with SVr4 _c_u_r_s_e_s.  As of
           2018, NetBSD _c_u_r_s_e_s also returns the number of  successful  conver‐
           sions.   Both _n_c_u_r_s_e_s and NetBSD _c_u_r_s_e_s call _v_s_s_c_a_n_f(3) to scan the
           string, which returns EEOOFF on error.

       •   Portable applications should test only if the return value is  EERRRR,
           and not compare it to OOKK, since that value (zero) might be mislead‐
           ing.

           One portable way to get useful results would be to use a “%n”  con‐
           version at the end of the format string, and check the value of the
           corresponding variable to determine how many conversions succeeded.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       ssccaannww was implemented in 4BSD (November 1980); that  early  version  of
       _c_u_r_s_e_s  preceded  the ANSI C standard of 1989.  The function was unused
       in Berkeley distributions for over ten years, until 4.4BSD,  which  em‐
       ployed it in a game.  The 4BSD ssccaannww did not use _v_a_r_a_r_g_s_._h, though that
       had been available since Seventh Edition Unix (1979).  In 1991 (a  cou‐
       ple  of years after SVr4 was generally available, and after the C stan‐
       dard was  published),  other  developers  updated  the  library,  using
       _s_t_d_a_r_g_._h  internally in 4.4BSD _c_u_r_s_e_s.  Even with this improvement, BSD
       _c_u_r_s_e_s did not use function prototypes (nor even declare functions)  in
       _c_u_r_s_e_s_._h until 1992.

       SVr2  (1984)  documented  ssccaannww  and  wwssccaannww  tersely as “scanf through
       ssttddssccrr” and “scanf through _w_i_n”, respectively.

       SVr3 (1987) added mmvvssccaannww, and mmvvwwssccaannww, stating

              “[t]hese routines correspond to _s_c_a_n_f(3S), as do their arguments
              and  return  values.  wwggeettssttrr() is called on the window, and the
              resulting line is used as input for the scan.”

       SVr3 also implemented vvwwssccaannww, describing  its  third  parameter  as  a
       _v_a___l_i_s_t,  defined  in  _v_a_r_a_r_g_s_._h, and referred the reader to the manual
       pages for _v_a_r_a_r_g_s and _v_p_r_i_n_t_f for detailed descriptions.  (Because  the
       SVr3  documentation  does  not mention _v_s_c_a_n_f, the reference to _v_p_r_i_n_t_f
       might not be an error).

       SVr4 (1989) introduced no new variations of _s_c_a_n_w, but provided for us‐
       ing either _v_a_r_a_r_g_s_._h or _s_t_d_a_r_g_._h to define the _v_a___l_i_s_t type.

       X/Open  Curses,  Issue  4  (1995), defined _v_w___s_c_a_n_w to replace _v_w_s_c_a_n_w,
       stating that its _v_a___l_i_s_t type is defined in _s_t_d_a_r_g_._h.

